
#import "../../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Fargues–Fontaine curve",
))

We're aiming to define the Fargues–Fontaine curve geometrizing the Frobenius orbits of untilts of an algebraically closed perfectoid field of characteristic $p$ and study its properties.

#subtree(
  title: "untilts",
  taxon: "section"
)[
Recall that there is an equivalence of categories:
$
  C_"fét" = C^flat_"fét"
$
between the category of finite étale algebras over a perfectoid field $C$ the category of finite étale algebras over its tilt $C^flat$. In particular, $C$ is algebraically closed if and only if $C^flat$ is algebraically closed. 

Let $(C^flat, cal(O)_C^flat, frak(m)_C^flat)$ be a perfectoid field of characteristic $p$. Every untilt of $cal(O)_C^flat$ comes from the quotient of $AA_"inf" := W(cal(O)_C^flat)$ by a principal ideal generated by a distinguished element $xi$, i.e. an element of the form $p + [pi] u$ for some $pi in frak(m)_C^flat$ and $u in AA_"inf"^times$. The distinguished element of the untilt $C$ can be canonically chosen in the following cases:
#theorem()[
  - There is an bijection between $frak(m)_C^flat without {0}$ and characteristic $0$ untilt $C$ equipped with $iota : QQ_(p)[p^(p^(-infinity))] -> C$.
  - There is an bijection between $frak(m)_C^flat without {0}$ and characteristic $0$ untilt $C$ equipped with $iota : QQ_(p)^"cyc" -> C$.
]

#proof()[
  The distinguished elements are constructed as follows:
  $
    p^flat & = (p, p^(p^(-1)), p^(p^(-2)), ...) in (QQ_(p)[p^(p^(-infinity))])^flat \
    xi & = p + [p^flat] \
    epsilon & = (1, zeta_p, zeta_(p^2), ...) in (QQ_(p)^"cyc")^flat \
    xi & = 1 + [epsilon^(1/p)] + [epsilon^(2/p)] + ... + [epsilon^(1-1/p)] \
  $
  We only need to prove that $1 + [epsilon^(1/p)] + [epsilon^(2/p)] + ... + [epsilon^(1-1/p)]$ is distinguished, which follows from modulo $frak(m)_C^flat$ and modulo $p$ computations in $AA_"inf"$. Hence the construction $p^flat$ and $epsilon-1$ induces the desired bijections.
]

The Galois group $ZZ_p^times$ of $QQ_p^"cyc" slash QQ_p$ acts on $epsilon$ by $ZZ_p^times$-th power via $iota : QQ_p^"cyc" -> C$ and the Frobenius action on $C^flat$ acts on $epsilon$ by $p^(ZZ)$-th power. Then

#subtree(
  taxon: "corollary"
)[
  - There is a bijection between untilts of $C^flat$ containing $QQ_p^"cyc"$ and $ZZ_p^times$-orbits in $frak(m)_C^flat without {0}$.
  - There is a bijection between Frobenius orbits of untilts of $C^flat$ containing $QQ_p^"cyc"$ and $QQ_p^times$-orbits in $frak(m)_C^flat without {0}$.
]
Let $Y$ be the set of all characteristic $0$ untilts of $C^flat$ and $overline(Y) := Y union {0}$ be the set of all untilts of $C^flat$. 
#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  If $C^flat$ is algebraically closed, $overline(Y)$ is a complete ultrametric space with metric defined by:
  $
    d(x, y) = norm(xi_(x)(y))_(C_y)
  $
]
#proof()[
  - $xi_x$ may differ by a unit in $AA_"inf"$, which maps to $cal(O)_(C_y)^times$, so $d$ is independent of the choice of $xi_x$ and well-defined. Moreover, $d(x, y) = 0$ if and only if $x = y$.
  - For $x, y in overline(Y)$, there exists $c in C^flat$ such that $c^sharp = xi_(x)(y) in C_y$ due to the surjection $(-)^(sharp) : C^flat -> C$. Here we use $C_y$ is algebraically closed. Then $xi_y = xi_x - [c]$ and:
  $
    d(x, y) = norm(c)_(C^flat) = d(y, x)
  $
  - For $x, y, z in overline(Y)$, we may pick $xi_z = xi_y - [c']$ and $xi_y = xi_x - [c]$. Then:
  $
    d(x, z) = norm(c^sharp + c'^sharp)_(C_z) <= max {norm(c^sharp)_(C_z), norm(c'^sharp)_(C_z)} = max {d(x, y), d(y, z)}
  $
  - For $x_n in overline(Y)$ Cauchy with respect to $d$, we may pick $xi_(x_n) = xi_(x_(n-1)) - [c_(n-1)]$ such that $c_(n-1)$ converges to $0$ in $C^flat$. The limit $xi_x := lim xi_(x_n)$ exists and is a distinguished element of $AA_"inf"$. Then $x$ is the desired limit of $x_n$.
]
#subtree(
  taxon: "remark"
)[
  $overline(Y)$ is analogues to the complex disc $D := {z in CC mid(|) abs(z) < 1}$ as follows:
  - $p$ is analogues to $z$
  - $AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ is analogues to meromorphic functions on $D$ with a possible pole at $z = 0$, represented by $p$-power series.
  - To allow essential singularity at $0$, we have to introduce the period ring $BB$.
]

]

#subtree(
  title: "Gauß norm",
  taxon: "section"
)[
#subtree(
  title: "Gauß norm",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  For each $s > 0$, or equivalently, $0 < rho < 1$, where $rho = exp(-s)$, we define a Gauß norm with corresponding valuation on $AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ by:
  $
    norm(sum_(n >> - infinity) [c_n] p^n)_rho & := sup {norm(c_n)_(C^flat) rho^n} \
    v_(s)(sum_(n >> - infinity) [c_n] p^n) & := inf {v_(C^flat)(c_n) + n s} \
    & = - log norm(sum_(n >> - infinity) [c_n] p^n)_exp(-s)
  $
  The valuation defines a concave polygon $s mapsto v_(s)(f)$ for every non-zero series:
  $
    f = sum_(n >> - infinity) [c_n] p^n in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]
  $
  The Gauß norm is compatible with the Frobenius automorphism $phi$ on $AA_("inf")$ sending $[c]$ to $[c^p]$ in the sense that:
  $
    norm(phi(f))_(rho^p) & = (norm(f)_(rho))^p \
    v_(p s)(phi(f)) & = p v_(s)(f)
  $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  Fix $0 < a <= b < 1$, we define $BB_[a,b]$ as the completion of $AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ with respect to the Gauß norm $norm(-)_a$ and $norm(-)_b$, or equivalently, all Gauß norms $norm(-)_rho$ for $a <= rho <= b$. We define $BB$ as the completion of $AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ with respect to all Gauß norms $norm(-)_rho$ for $0 < rho < 1$, or equivalently, the inverse limit of all $BB_[a,b]$. In particular, the Frobenius automorphism $phi$ on $AA_("inf")$ extends to an automorphism of $BB$ and induces an isomorphism $BB_[a,b] = BB_[a^p, b^p]$ for every $0 < a <= b < 1$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  Fix $x in Y$ with $rho := d(0,x) = norm(p)_(C_x)$. We define:
  $
    BB_"dR"^+ = lim_(n) (AA_"inf" / (xi_x)^n)[p^(-1)]
  $
  Then $BB_"dR"^+$ is a complete discrete valuation field with uniformizer $xi_x$ and residue field $C$. We define $BB_"dR" := BB_"dR"^+[xi^(-1)]$. The evaluation map $AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]] -> C$ decompose as:
  $
    AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]] -> BB_[a,b] -> BB_"dR"^+ -> C
  $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "lemma"
)[
  Let $f_n in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ such that $f_n -> f in BB_([exp(-p), exp(-q)])$ with $f != 0$, then the polygon $s mapsto v_(s)(f)$ on $[q, p]$, the slope $partial_+ v_(p)(f)$ and the slope $partial_- v_(q)(f)$ are eventually constant. In particular, the polygon and the slopes at the edges extends to $BB_([exp(-p), exp(-q)])$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "corollary"
)[
  $BB_([exp(-p), exp(-q)])$ is an integral domain. In particular, $BB$ is an integral domain.
]

#proof()[
  Every $f in BB_([exp(-p), exp(-q)])$ corresponds to a concave polygon on $[q, p]$ which is everywhere finite. The polygon of products is the sum of the polygons and is still everywhere finite.
]

#subtree(
  title: "structure theorem",
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  - If $f in BB_([exp(-p), exp(-q)])$ satisfy $partial_+ v_(p)(f) = partial_- v_(q)(f)$, then $f$ is a unit in $BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$.
  - If $C^flat$ is algebraically closed, $f in BB_([a, b])$ such that $f(x) = 0 in C_x$ for some $x in Y$ with $rho := d(0,x)$ satisfying $a <= rho <= b$, then $f = xi_x g$ for some $g in BB_([a, b])$.
  - The polygon of $xi_x$ has a unique break point at $s = - log rho$ with slope $1$ on the left and slope $0$ on the right. 
  - If $C^flat$ is algebraically closed, $f in BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ such that $partial_+ v_(s)(f) < partial_- v_(s)(f)$, then $f$ vanishes at some $x in Y$ with $d(0,x) = exp(-s)$. In particular, $BB_([a, b])$ is a principal ideal domain with each non-zero prime ideal generated by $xi_x$ for some $x in Y$ with $rho := d(0,x)$ satisfying $a <= rho <= b$. 
]

#proof()[
  - We first assume that $f in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$, then the polygon is linear on $[alpha, beta]$ for $alpha < q$ and $beta > p$, controlled by $[c_n] p^n$. Then
    $
      f^(-1) = [c_n^(-1)] p^(-n) sum_(k=0)^(infinity) (1 - [c_n^(-1)] p^(-n) f)^(k)
    $
    If $f_n -> f$ then $f_n^(-1)$ exists for $n >> 0$ by eventually constancy and converges to $f^(-1)$.
  - Every $f in BB_([a,b])$ such that $f(x) = 0$ can be approximated by $f_n in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ such that $f_(n)(x) = 0$, then $xi^(-1) f_n$ converges to $xi^(-1) f$. For example, $f_n := g_n - [g_(n)(x)^flat]$ with $g_n -> f$.
  - Trivial.
  - We first prove for primitive $f in AA_"inf"$, that is:
    $
      f = sum_(n >= 0) [c_n] p^n
    $
    such that $c_0 != 0$ and $norm(c_d)_(C^flat) = 1$. The smallest $d$ in $norm(c_d)_(C^flat) = 1$ is the degree of a primitive element. Let $d$ be the degree of $f$. Since $C^flat$ is algebraically closed:
    $
      c_d x^d + ... + c_0 = c_d (x - r_1) ... (x - r_d) in C^(flat)[x] \
      norm(r_1)_(C^flat) <= ... <= norm(r_d)_(C^flat)
    $
    A straightforward calculation of polygon shows there exists $n$ such that 
    $
      norm(r_n)_(C^flat) = exp(-s)
    $
    We begin induction with $xi_x_1 = p - [r_n]$. As $AA_"inf"$ is $xi_x$-adically complete and $C^flat$ surjects onto $C := AA_"inf" slash xi_x$, every element can be written as:
    $
      f = sum_(n >= 0) [b_n] xi_x^n \
      f(x) = b_0^sharp in C_x
    $
    A reduction to $W(cal(O)_(C)^flat slash frak(m)_C^flat)$ shows $norm(b_d)_(C^flat) = 1$ and $norm(b_n)_(C^flat) < 1$ for all $n < d$. If $xi = xi_x_1$:
    $
      [c_n^(-1)] p^(-n) b_0 = [c_n^(-1)] p^(-n) f = sum_(i = 0)^(d) ((c_i r_n^(n-i)) / c_n)^sharp = 0 in p cal(O)_(C_x)
    $
    So we begin with $norm(b_0)_(C^flat) <= exp(-(d+1)s)$. Now fix $xi_x$ such that $norm(b_0)_(C^flat) < exp(-d s)$ and $d(0, x) >= exp(-s)$. Since $C^flat$ is algebraically closed:
    $
      b_d x^d + ... + b_0 = b_d (x - r_1) ... (x - r_d) in C^(flat)[x] \
      norm(r_1)_(C^flat) <= ... <= norm(r_d)_(C^flat)
    $
    In particular, $norm(r_1)_(C^flat) <= norm(b_0)_(C^flat)^(d^(-1)) < exp(-s)$. Let $xi_y = xi_x - [r_1]$ then:
    $
      [b_0]^(-1) f = sum_(i = 0)^(d) (r_1^(i)/b_0)^sharp = 0 in cal(O)_(C_y)
    $
    Then $norm(f(y))_(C^flat) <= norm(f(x))_(C^flat) exp(-s)$. In conclusion, for every $xi_(x_k)$ with 
    $
      d(0, x_k) >= exp(-s) \
      norm(f(x_k))_(C^flat) < exp(-d s)
    $
    there exists $xi_x_(k+1)$ with 
    $
      d(x_k,x_(k+1)) <= norm(f(x))_(C^flat) \ 
      norm(f(x_(k+1)))_(C^flat) <= norm(f(x))_(C^flat) exp(-s)
    $
    The series $x_k$ is Cauchy and coverges to $x$ in $Y$ with $d(0,x) = exp(-s)$, which is the required $x$.

    To extend from primitive $f$ to all $f$, we first note that if $f = g h$ is primitive, then $g, h$ is still primitive with $deg f = deg g + deg h$. Hence every primitive $f$ of degree $d$ admits a factorization:
    $
      f = u xi_(x_1) ... xi_(x_d), u in AA_"inf"^times
    $
    Every finite Teichmüller series can be written as $sum_(n=-N)^(M) [c_n] p^n = p^m [c] f$ with $f$ primitive. We'll call $f in BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ good if $f$ admits a factorization 
    $
      f = u xi_(x_1) ... xi_(x_d), u in BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])^times
    $
    then every finite Teichmüller series is good. When $f$ is good:
    $
      norm(f(y))_(C_y) = norm(u(y))_(C_y) product d(y,x_i) = norm(u)_(d(0,y)) product d(y,x_i) = norm(f)_(d(0,y)) product d(y,x_i)/d(0,y)
    $
    If $f in BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ is good, given $g in BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ such that 
    $
      norm(f - g)_(exp(-s)) < norm(f)_(exp(-s))
    $
    and $y$ with $d(0,y) = exp(-s)$ such that $g(y) = 0$:
    $
      norm(f)_(exp(-s)) > norm(f - g)_(exp(-s)) >= norm(f)_(exp(-s)) product_(i=1)^n d(y,x_i)/exp(-s)
    $
    In particular, there exists at least one $x_i$ such that
    $
      d(y,x_i) < exp(-s) (norm(f - g)_(exp(-s))/norm(f)_(exp(-s)))^(d^(-1))
    $
    We can now conclude that if $f_i -> f$ in $BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ such that $f_i$ is good, for example, every $BB_([exp(-s), exp(-s)])$ can be approximated by finite Teichmüller series, then $f$ vanishes at some $y$ with $d(0, y) = exp(-s)$. After $v_(s)(f_i), partial_+ v_(s)(f_i), partial_- v_(s)(f_i)$ are constant, we may inductively pick $y_i$ with $d(0, y_i) = exp(-s)$ such that:
    $
      d(y_(i+1), y_i) < exp(-s) (norm(f_(i+1) - f_i)_(exp(-s))/norm(f)_(exp(-s)))^(d^(-1))
    $
    Hence $y_i$ is Cauchy and converges to a zero of $f$.
]
]

#subtree(
  title: "Fargues–Fontaine curve",
  taxon: "section"
)[
#subtree(
  title: "Fargues–Fontaine curve",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  Let $BB^(phi = p^n)$ denotes the subset of $BB$ consisting of elements $x$ such that $phi(x) = p^n x$, where $phi$ is the Frobenius automorphism on $BB$. The Fargues–Fontaine curve is defined as the Proj:
  $
    X_"FF" := op("Proj")(plus.o.big_(n>=0) BB^(phi = p^n))
  $
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "lemma"
)[
  Let $f$ be a non-zero element in $BB$, $norm(f)_rho <= 1$ for all $rho in (0,1)$ if and only if $f in AA_"inf"$. In particular,
  - there exists $C > 0$ such that $norm(f)_rho <= C$ for all $rho in [0,1]$ if and only if $f in AA_("inf")[[pi^(-1)]]$.
  - there exists $n < 0$ such that $norm(f)_rho <= rho^n$ for all $rho in [0,1]$ if and only if $f in AA_("inf")[p^(-1)]$.
  - there exists $C > 0$ and $n < 0$ such that $norm(f)_rho <= C rho^n$ for all $rho in [0,1]$ if and only if $f in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$.
]

#proof()[
  We prove that there exists $c in cal(O)_C^flat$ such that $norm(f - [c])_(rho) <= rho$ for all $rho$. There exists $f_i in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]$ such that $f_i -> f$ in $BB$. Pick $f_i^+ in AA_("inf")[[pi^(-1)]]$
  $
    f_i^+ & := sum_(n >= 0) [c_(i,n)] p^n \
    f_i & = sum_(n >> -infinity) [c_(i,n)] p^n
  $
  Note that $norm(f_i^+)_(rho) <= norm(f_i)_(rho)$ for all $rho$. For any $0 < epsilon < 1$, we have inequality for $i >> 0$:
  $
    norm(f_i - f_i^+)_(rho) <= epsilon norm(f_i - f_i^+)_(epsilon rho) <= epsilon
  $ 
  Hence $f_i^+ -> f$. The Teichmüller series of $f_i^+ - f_j^+$ begin with $[c_(i,0) - c_(j,0)]$. Note that:
  $
    norm(c_(i,0) - c_(j,0))_(C^flat) <= norm(f_i^+ - f_j^+)_(rho) \
    norm(c_(i,0))_(C^flat) <= norm(f_i^+)_(rho)
  $
  Now $c_(i,0)$ is Cauchy and converges to some $c in cal(O)_(C)^flat$. It'll suffices to prove $norm(f - [c])_(rho) <= rho$ for all $rho$. Note that every slope of the polygon $v_(s)(f_i^+ - [c_(i,0)])$ is greater than $1$ and that $v_(s)(f_i^+ - [c_(i,0)]) >= 0$ for fixed $s$ and $i >> 0$. In particular, $v_(s)(f_i^+ - [c_(i,0)]) >= s - epsilon$ for every fixed $epsilon$ and $i >> 0$. Hence the limit $f - [c]$ satisfy $v_(s)(f - [c]) >= s$.

  Apply the above argument inductively to $p^(-1)(f - [c])$, there exists $c_k$ such that for all $n$:
  $
    norm(f - sum_(k=0)^n [c_n] p^n)_(rho) <= rho^(n+1)
  $
  Hence $f = sum_(k=0)^(infinity) [c_n] p^n$ in $AA_"inf"$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  - $BB^(phi = p^(-n)) = 0$ for $n > 0$.
  - $BB^(phi = 1) = QQ_p$.
  - Every $f in BB^(phi = p^n)$ for $n > 0$ admits a factorization:
    $
      f = u log([epsilon_1]) ... log([epsilon_n])
    $
    where $u in QQ_p^times$ and $epsilon_i$ are unique up to $QQ_p^times$.
  - $BB^(phi = p) = 1 + frak(m)_C^flat$ via the logrithm map.
  - The Fargues–Fontaine curve is a Dedekind scheme whose closed points are in bijection to Frobenius orbits of characteristic $0$ untilts.
]

#proof()[
  If $phi(f) = p^n f$, the derivative of $v_(p s)(phi(f)) = p v_(s)(f)$:
  $
    n + p (partial v)_(p s)(f) = p (partial v)_(s)(f)
  $
  - Contradicts to $v_(s)(f)$ concave. 
  - $v_(s)(f)$ must be linear, we may assume:
    $
      f = sum_(n >> - infinity) [c_n] p^n in AA_("inf")[p^(-1), [pi^(-1)]]
    $
    then $c_n^p = c_n$, showing $c_n in FF_p$ and $f in QQ_p$.
  - The logrithm map $log([-]) : 1 + frak(m)_C^flat -> B^(phi = p)$ is given by:
    $
      log([epsilon]) := sum_(k > 0) (-1)^(k+1)/k ([epsilon] - 1)^k
    $
    Let $epsilon != 1$. Then $log([epsilon])(x) = 0 in C_x$ if $x$ is on the Frobenius orbits of characteristic $0$ untilts of $C^flat$ corresponding to the $QQ_p^times$-orbits of $frak(m)_C^flat without {0}$ for $epsilon - 1$. If $f in BB^(phi = p^n)$, $f$ has some zeros due to $n + p (partial v)_(p s)(f) = p (partial v)_(s)(f)$. Now $f slash log([epsilon']) in BB^(phi = p^(n-1))$ for some $epsilon' in 1 + frak(m)_C^flat$. Conclude using induction and $B^(phi = 1) = QQ_p$.
  - Trivial.
  - Trivial.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "remark"
)[
  The topology of adic space Fargues–Fontaine curve equals the quotient $Y slash phi^ZZ$ equipped with quotient topology.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "corollary"
)[
  The Picard group of $X$ is equal to $ZZ$ via $scr(O)(1)$.
]

]

#subtree(
  title: "Geometry of Fargues–Fontaine curve"
)[
We now assume that $C^flat$ is algebraically closed. We use $X_("FF", E)$ for $X_"FF" times.o_QQ_p E$, where $E$ is a finite extension of $QQ_p$ with ramification degree $e$ and inertia degree $f$.
#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  For every closed point $x$ on $X_E$ corresponding to a $phi^(f ZZ)$-orbit $S subset Y_E$, there exists a non-zero $f in (BB times.o_QQ_p E)^(phi^f = pi)$ vanishing on $S$, where $pi$ is a uniformizer of $E$. In particular, the Picard group of $X_E$ equal to $ZZ$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  The opposite category of isoclinic isocrystals of slope $mu$ is equivalent to the category of semistable vector bundles of slope $mu$.
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  The Fargues–Fontaine curve is geometrically simply connected:
  $
    pi_(1)(X_"FF" times.o_QQ_p CC_p) = 0
  $
]
]

